{"id": "objectives/empowerment-the-drive-for-control/computational-requirements-and-neuro-evolutionary-implementation/1-an-intrinsic-motivational-signal", "title": "(1) An intrinsic motivational signal", "group": "objectives", "parent": "objectives/empowerment-the-drive-for-control/computational-requirements-and-neuro-evolutionary-implementation", "source": "2a_objectives.tex", "line": 384, "children": [], "level": 4, "domain": "", "part": "", "construction": false, "editorialCount": 0, "excerpt": "(1) An intrinsic motivational signal: empowerment as a dopaminergically encoded drive. Empowerment cannot shape behavior unless the organism has some way to value increases in control: the system must convert \"this state affords many reliably distinct futures\"", "html": "<div class=\"manuscript-children\">\n<div class=\"manuscript-paragraph\"><p><strong>(1) An intrinsic motivational signal: empowerment as a dopaminergically encoded drive.</strong></p><div class=\"manuscript-children\">\n<div class=\"manuscript-paragraph\"><p>Empowerment cannot shape behavior unless the organism has some way to <em>value</em> increases in control: the system must convert \"this state affords many reliably distinct futures\" into an intrinsically appetitive signal that biases attention, exploration, and action selection. Classical motivation theories propose that organisms are not only driven by homeostatic deficits but also by an \"effectance/competence\" tendency to seek mastery and causal efficacy, and later work frames autonomy/competence as basic psychological needs–suggesting an <em>evolution-installed</em> preference for being an effective cause in the world, even when immediate extrinsic reward is absent <sup class=\"citation\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" title=\"Motivation Reconsidered: The Concept of Competence\">1</a>, <a href=\"#ref-2\" title=\"Self-Determination Theory and the Facilitation of Intrinsic Motivation, Social Development, and Well-Being\">2</a></sup>. Empirically, infants rapidly learn action<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>outcome contingencies in paradigms like the mobile conjugate reinforcement task and show physiological/affective reactivity when contingency is violated (nonreinforcement), consistent with an early-emerging valuation of controllability (though this does not, by itself, resolve whether it is present \"from birth\" versus learned extremely quickly) <sup class=\"citation\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" title=\"The mobile conjugate reinforcement paradigm in a lab setting\">3</a>, <a href=\"#ref-4\" title=\"Contingency Learning and Reactivity in Preterm and Full-Term Infants\">4</a></sup>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"manuscript-paragraph\"><p>Importantly, even if the initial bias is weak or purely \"curiosity-like,\" <strong>dopaminergic TD learning can rapidly bootstrap empowerment into a proxy for long-run reward</strong>: states and policies that <em>expand</em> controllable futures tend to (i) increase the chance of encountering extrinsic rewards, (ii) enable flexible switching when goals change, and (iii) reduce the expected cost of adverse events–so repeated experience causes value to back up onto high-empowerment regions of state space (see \"<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/article/fundamentals/neuromodulators\">Neuromodulators</a>\" for TD/RPE details and how evolution shaped dopaminergic circuits) <sup class=\"citation\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" title=\"Dopamine prediction errors in reward learning and addiction\">5</a>, <a href=\"#ref-6\" title=\"Tonic dopamine: opportunity costs and the control of response vigor\">6</a></sup>. Recent evidence that dopamine can carry value-free <em>action</em>-prediction errors (teaching which actions reliably produce which outcomes, not only which outcomes are rewarding) further supports a mechanistic bridge from \"learn the channel\" to \"value the channel\" <sup class=\"citation\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" title=\"Dopaminergic action prediction errors serve as a value-free teaching signal\">7</a>, <a href=\"#ref-8\" title=\"Dopamine in Motivational Control: Rewarding, Aversive, and Alerting\">8</a></sup>. The dual-controller model separates two candidate teaching signals (<a href=\"#figure-learning-pmc12310545-fig5\">Figure</a>). The experiment underlying this dual-controller account uses an auditory discrimination task (<a href=\"#figure-paper-pmc12310545-fig1\">Figure</a>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"figure-row\"><figure class=\"paper-figure\" id=\"figure-learning-pmc12310545-fig5\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/learning-pmc12310545-fig5.jpg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"A dual-controller model distinguishes value-based and action-prediction-error learning. The schematic and model tests connect dopamine signals to acquisition of state–action associations.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/learning-pmc12310545-fig5.jpg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">A dual-controller model distinguishes value-based and action-prediction-error learning. The schematic and model tests connect dopamine signals to acquisition of state–action associations.</p><blockquote class=\"source-caption\"><p>“A dual-controller model predicts the effect of experimental manipulations. <b>a</b>, Schematic of the network model. <b>b</b>, Top, task performance across learning for the full dual-controller model (combined), the value-based controller, or the value-free controller. Bottom, differences in performance between the combined model and the value-based model (12 random agents selected for each). <b>c</b>, Change of the model weights for a reward association (high tone→left action), as means for 100 agents. Vertical lines indicate inactivation time points in <b>d</b>. <b>d</b>, Performance levels before and after (as the mean after ten trials) model inactivations of the TS or the actor networks. <b>e</b>, Schematic of the experimental approach for acute inhibition of D1 SPNs or D2 SPNs in the TS. <b>f</b>, Quantification of the contralateral bias on opto-stimulated trials (Methods) for each session as a function of session’s performance. Error bars represent 95% confidence intervals. Lines show the mean and s.d. of linear fits for each mouse in each dataset. D1-Arch P = 0.004; A2A-Arch P = 7.6 × 10<sup>−4</sup>; Methods; n = 8 mice. <b>g</b>, Proportion of significantly biased sessions in <b>f</b> as a function of performance. <b>h</b>, Final weights in the TS for each sound–action association.Source data”</p></blockquote><p class=\"credit\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12310545/#Fig5\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Greenstreet et al., 2025, Nature, Fig. 5</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">CC BY 4.0</a></p></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"paper-figure\" id=\"figure-paper-pmc12310545-fig1\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/paper-pmc12310545-fig1.jpg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Mouse auditory discrimination task and tail-of-striatum manipulation.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/paper-pmc12310545-fig1.jpg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">The study uses an auditory discrimination task to examine the contribution of posterior striatum.</p><blockquote class=\"source-caption\"><p>“TS is needed to facilitate learning and for execution of the auditory discrimination task.a, Schematic of the task. Frequencies (freq.) represent auditory stimuli, volumes represent reward. b, Muscimol injection locations, indicated by the co-injection of fluorescent cholera toxin B. Scale bar, 2 mm. c, Psychometric task performance of mice, saline in TS (2 mice; 4 sessions), DMS (3 mice; 11 sessions) and TS (5 mice; 15 sessions). Lines represent logistic fits of the means. d, Schematic for inhibition of D1 SPNs or D2 SPNs. e, Psychometric task performance for opto-stimulated trials for the D1 (Drd1-cre) archaeorhodopsin (Arch) (8 mice; 15 sessions) and A2A (Adora2a-cre) Arch (8 mice; 12 sessions) mice. Lines represent logistic fits of the means. Stim, stimulation. f, Quantification of the bias for each session shown in e. Scatter dots represent the mean for each session, and error bars illustrate the variation of shuffled data (Methods). Colour-filled dots indicate P &lt; 0.05. D1-Arch: P = 2.27 × 10−5, Cohen’s d = −1.40; A2A-Arch: P = 2 × 10−7, Cohen’s d = 2.33; Kruskal–Wallis test. g, Example lesion and control mice histology. h, Learning rate of the lesioned TS (n = 11 mice) and control (n = 10 mice) groups (lines represents group means). i,j, Maximum performance (i; P = 0.0023, Kruskal–Wallis test; Cohen’s d = −1.70) and maximum learning rate (j; P = 0.0006, Kruskal–Wallis test; Cohen’s d = −2.04) between the groups (same mice as in h). k, Example TH staining in the TS for control and lesion mice. l, Learning rate of the TS dopamine-ablated (n = 9 mice) and control (n = 5 mice) groups. m,n, Differences on the maximum performance (m; P = 0.006, Kruskal–Wallis test; Cohen’s d = −2.39) and maximum learning rate (n; P = 0.014, Kruskal–Wallis test; Cohen’s d = −1.71) between the groups (same mice as l). Error bars indicate s.d. in c,e,h,l. In box plots, boxes represent quartiles 2 and 3, the centre line shows the median and whiskers extend to the furthest data point within 1.5 times the inter-quartile range from the box.Source data”</p></blockquote><p class=\"credit\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12310545/#Fig1\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Greenstreet et al., 2025, Fig. 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">CC BY 4.0</a></p></figcaption></figure></div></div></div>\n</div>\n", "references": ["Motivation Reconsidered: The Concept of Competence", "Self-Determination Theory and the Facilitation of Intrinsic Motivation, Social Development, and Well-Being", "The mobile conjugate reinforcement paradigm in a lab setting", "Contingency Learning and Reactivity in Preterm and Full-Term Infants", "Dopamine prediction errors in reward learning and addiction", "Tonic dopamine: opportunity costs and the control of response vigor", "Dopaminergic action prediction errors serve as a value-free teaching signal", "Dopamine in Motivational Control: Rewarding, Aversive, and Alerting"], "toc": [], "words": 285, "minutes": 1, "status": "draft", "referenceDetails": [null, null, {"title": "The mobile conjugate reinforcement paradigm in a lab setting.", "authors": ["Emily C Merz", "Laraine McDonough", "Yong Lin Huang", "Sophie Foss", "Elizabeth Werner", "Catherine Monk"], "year": "2017", "journal": "Developmental psychobiology", "doi": "10.1002/dev.21520", "pmid": "28436585", "source": "Europe PMC"}, {"title": "Contingency Learning and Reactivity in Preterm and Full-Term Infants at 3 Months.", "authors": ["David W Haley", "Ruth E Grunau", "Tim F Oberlander", "Joanne Weinberg"], "year": "2008", "journal": "Infancy : the official journal of the International Society on Infant Studies", "doi": "10.1080/15250000802458682", "pmid": "20717491", "source": "Europe PMC"}, {"title": "Dopamine Prediction Errors in Reward Learning and Addiction: From Theory to Neural Circuitry.", "authors": ["Ronald Keiflin", "Patricia H Janak"], "year": "2015", "journal": "Neuron", "doi": "10.1016/j.neuron.2015.08.037", "pmid": "26494275", "source": "Europe PMC"}, {"title": "Tonic dopamine: opportunity costs and the control of response vigor.", "authors": ["Yael Niv", "Nathaniel D Daw", "Daphna Joel", "Peter Dayan"], "year": "2007", "journal": "Psychopharmacology", "doi": "10.1007/s00213-006-0502-4", "pmid": "17031711", "source": "Europe PMC"}, {"title": "Dopaminergic action prediction errors serve as a value-free teaching signal.", "authors": ["Francesca Greenstreet", "Hernando Martinez Vergara", "Yvonne Johansson", "Sthitapr